1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,925
[Woman's Voice]
Cat got your tongue?

2
00:00:06,002 --> 00:00:08,938
No. You killed the cat.
Cut offhis head.

3
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:11,133
[Woman]
Need to take a look at this.

4
00:00:11,210 --> 00:00:14,112
Mom? Mom.

5
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:16,205
Mom, it's okay.

6
00:00:18,252 --> 00:00:23,122
Just a couple of questions
before I can authorize extending
her disability benefits.

7
00:00:23,192 --> 00:00:25,786
I don't like her.
She's fat.

8
00:00:27,198 --> 00:00:30,760
- I could lose a little weight.
- [Gasps]

9
00:00:36,878 --> 00:00:38,505
You all right?

10
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:43,245
- Actually, before she signs-
- I killed the cat. Lots of blood.

11
00:00:44,989 --> 00:00:47,048
It's okay.

12
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:50,152
I have a couple of questions
about some of these dates.

13
00:00:50,229 --> 00:00:53,165
- The first diagnosis-
- Schizophrenia.

14
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:56,328
Dr. Walters, May 11, last year.
The letter's in the medical file.

15
00:00:56,404 --> 00:00:59,340
And April 6-
that was the last day she worked?

16
00:00:59,408 --> 00:01:02,845
But she received unemployment
benefits for that week.

17
00:01:02,913 --> 00:01:06,350
We fixed that. I know we did.
We returned the money.

18
00:01:10,423 --> 00:01:12,254
That's the canceled check.

19
00:01:12,325 --> 00:01:14,885
Not the real one, you know.
It's a copy.

20
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:16,896
And you're the dependent?

21
00:01:16,965 --> 00:01:21,801
No. That's my little brother.
I'm 18.Just helping out.

22
00:01:26,611 --> 00:01:28,545
You're all set.

23
00:01:28,614 --> 00:01:30,479
Just need a signature.

24
00:01:30,550 --> 00:01:33,246
[Woman's Voice]
Hey.! I'm talking to you.!

25
00:01:33,321 --> 00:01:36,758
The cat's first.
Now it's your turn, Lucy.

26
00:01:36,825 --> 00:01:39,055
Shut up.
Shut up, shut up!

27
00:01:40,163 --> 00:01:44,498
It's okay. Shejust-
Shejust needs a little water is all.

28
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:46,559
I'll go get it.

29
00:01:51,144 --> 00:01:53,669
Just hold on, okay?
Just-

30
00:01:53,748 --> 00:01:58,243
When she comes back,
sign it and we're gonna be done.
We need this, okay? Please.

31
00:02:00,457 --> 00:02:02,925
[Sighs]
Okay.

32
00:02:02,994 --> 00:02:05,019
The voices-

33
00:02:05,097 --> 00:02:07,088
The voices aren't real.

34
00:02:08,901 --> 00:02:10,892
[Gasps]

35
00:02:13,207 --> 00:02:15,198
What?

36
00:02:21,351 --> 00:02:23,512
[Gasping]

37
00:02:24,956 --> 00:02:26,947
Mom? Mom.

38
00:02:27,026 --> 00:02:30,053
Mom? Mom! Mom!

39
00:03:03,241 --> 00:03:07,303
[Man On Radio]
Thirty-eight-year-old Caucasian woman.
Status: Post-respiratory arrest in the field.

40
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:09,507
Intubated, oxygenating poorly.

41
00:03:10,617 --> 00:03:12,608
[Sighs]

42
00:03:16,959 --> 00:03:21,795
[Woman On P.A.] Dr. Gregory House,
please call Dr. Cuddy at extension 3731.

43
00:03:24,470 --> 00:03:27,929
This is a good hospital?

44
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:31,466
Depends what you mean by "good."
I like these chairs.

45
00:03:35,051 --> 00:03:36,951
How is she?
Stable. Okay.

46
00:03:37,019 --> 00:03:38,850
Your Mom had
a small pulmonary embolism-

47
00:03:38,922 --> 00:03:41,824
a blood clot
that got stuck in her lungs,
blocked the oxygen.

48
00:03:41,893 --> 00:03:46,092
But the pain started in her leg.
Where the clot started-
her calf.

49
00:03:46,166 --> 00:03:48,896
It's called a deep vein thrombosis.
Basically, a bigger clot.

50
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:51,906
It never hurt there before.
I would have noticed.

51
00:03:51,974 --> 00:03:55,741
A piece of that broke off, went up the vein,
through her heart and blocked
the blood flow to her lungs.

52
00:03:55,812 --> 00:03:57,541
No blood flow, no oxygen.
Okay.

53
00:03:57,615 --> 00:04:01,575
Is your Dad here? I have some things
I need to talk to him about.
Uh, my Dad's running a little late.

54
00:04:04,457 --> 00:04:07,393
He's dead. Just talk to me.
I take care of her.

55
00:04:07,461 --> 00:04:11,626
All right.
Your mom's blood alcohol was.12.
10:30 in the morning.

56
00:04:11,699 --> 00:04:14,566
I gave it to her.
Two ounces of vodka.
It cools her out.

57
00:04:14,638 --> 00:04:17,767
But that's the first since Monday.
That was three days ago.

58
00:04:17,842 --> 00:04:20,208
I've been real careful.

59
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:27,478
She hears voices.
She's schizophrenic.
That explains the D.V.T.

60
00:04:27,555 --> 00:04:30,251
The alcohol makes her pass out.
She's immobile
for long periods of time.

61
00:04:30,325 --> 00:04:33,489
That doesn't happen.
She's not an alcoholic.
She only drinks when you give it to her?

62
00:04:33,563 --> 00:04:37,000
We put her on blood thinners.
You can probably
take her home tomorrow.

63
00:04:37,067 --> 00:04:39,934
It's not the alcohol.
It's gotta be something else.

64
00:04:40,004 --> 00:04:43,133
Of course it's the alcohol.

65
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:46,677
Hello!

66
00:04:46,747 --> 00:04:49,375
This guy's a professional doctor.

67
00:04:49,451 --> 00:04:52,512
Plays golf and everything, I bet.

68
00:04:52,589 --> 00:04:56,492
He's not gonna tell you your mom's
an alcoholic without proof.

69
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:59,496
I'm sure he scoped for varices,
checked her esophagus,
ran all kinds of blood tests.

70
00:04:59,564 --> 00:05:02,466
Doctors like this,
they don't make assumptions.
They do the work.

71
00:05:02,535 --> 00:05:05,197
I'd be happy to refer you the case, Dr. House.
You seem so interested.

72
00:05:05,272 --> 00:05:08,333
What case? It's over.
You're sending her home.

73
00:05:08,409 --> 00:05:10,343
- How old is she?
- You're a doctor?

74
00:05:10,412 --> 00:05:11,845
Own my own stethoscope.

75
00:05:11,914 --> 00:05:14,816
Did I ask you how old she was?
I forget.

76
00:05:16,354 --> 00:05:19,290
A 38-year-old woman
with no previous symptoms or history...

77
00:05:19,358 --> 00:05:21,121
presents with deep vein thrombosis.

78
00:05:21,194 --> 00:05:22,957
How did she get it?

79
00:05:23,029 --> 00:05:25,862
Oral contraceptives, smoking, diabetes, obesity.
What's the point here?

80
00:05:25,933 --> 00:05:30,462
A D.V.T. Is a D.V.T.
Put her on I.V. Heparin
to prevent future clots.

81
00:05:30,539 --> 00:05:34,067
- What's the big mystery?
- Fine. You're all sleepy. You need a clue.

82
00:05:34,144 --> 00:05:37,205
She's 38 years old.

83
00:05:37,282 --> 00:05:40,149
She's 20 years too young
to get a deep vein thrombosis.

84
00:05:40,219 --> 00:05:43,815
I treated a 12-year-old girl once,
a soccer player. She got kicked in the leg.

85
00:05:43,890 --> 00:05:46,917
There was no trauma.
None of the risk factors.
You took a history?

86
00:05:46,995 --> 00:05:52,799
I have some notes.
They're not mine, but reliable, I think,
for the purposes of this discussion.

87
00:05:52,870 --> 00:05:57,604
As for the immobility, well,
she's real active right now, of course.
Paranoia keeps her limber.

88
00:05:57,676 --> 00:06:00,543
- Paranoia?
- Oh, yeah. She's schizophrenic.

89
00:06:00,613 --> 00:06:04,050
And her kid wrote this,
so it might be a little biased.

90
00:06:04,118 --> 00:06:07,179
You know, having to take care
of his nutso mom and all.

91
00:06:07,256 --> 00:06:09,588
You think there's a connection?

92
00:06:09,658 --> 00:06:13,095
- Do we include schizophrenia
in the differential for D. V.T.?
- Well-

93
00:06:13,164 --> 00:06:15,189
The answer is no.

94
00:06:15,266 --> 00:06:18,133
Abnormal dopaminergic pathways
in the brain do not cause blood clots.

95
00:06:18,203 --> 00:06:19,932
Schizophrenia
is not the cause of D.V.T.

96
00:06:20,006 --> 00:06:23,943
On the other hand, we don't really
know anything about schizophrenia,
so maybe it is connected.

97
00:06:24,011 --> 00:06:25,945
Well, schizophrenia
explains one mystery-

98
00:06:26,014 --> 00:06:28,950
why you're so fascinated by
a woman with a bump in her leg.

99
00:06:29,018 --> 00:06:32,455
It's like Picasso deciding
to whitewash a fence.
Thanks. I'm more of a Leroy Neiman man.

100
00:06:32,522 --> 00:06:35,458
And it is only about the D.V.T.
She's 38 years old. She shouldn't-

101
00:06:35,526 --> 00:06:39,019
Right, solve this one,
and you're on your way to Stockholm.
We don't even know how to treat it.

102
00:06:39,099 --> 00:06:41,033
Come on.
Fumigation of the vagina?

103
00:06:41,101 --> 00:06:44,037
A little louder.
I don't think everyone heard you.

104
00:06:44,105 --> 00:06:48,099
Two thousand years ago,
that's how Galen treated schizophrenics.
The Marcus Welby of ancient Greece.

105
00:06:48,177 --> 00:06:50,372
Oh, clearly
you're not interested.
Oh, I'm interested.

106
00:06:50,447 --> 00:06:55,317
I'm interested in how voices in the head
could be caused by malposition of the uterus.
There's a better place for it?

107
00:06:55,387 --> 00:06:58,323
Now, what do we got?
We've got lobotomies,
rubber rooms, electric shocks.

108
00:06:58,391 --> 00:07:00,951
My, Galen was so primitive.
Where are you going?

109
00:07:01,028 --> 00:07:05,363
Going to see the patient.
That all-important human connection.
Thought I'd give it a whirl.

110
00:07:05,434 --> 00:07:07,868
You won't talk to patients
because they lie,

111
00:07:07,937 --> 00:07:10,371
but give you a patient
with no concept of reality-

112
00:07:10,441 --> 00:07:14,071
If it wasn't for Socrates,
that raving, untreated schizophrenic,
we wouldn't have the Socratic method-

113
00:07:14,146 --> 00:07:17,583
the best way of teaching everything,
apart from juggling chain saws.

114
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:21,087
Without Isaac Newton,
we'd be floating on the ceiling.
Dodging chain saws, no doubt.

115
00:07:21,155 --> 00:07:24,887
And that guitar player
in that English band.
He was great.

116
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:28,021
You think I'm interested
because of the schizophrenia.

117
00:07:28,098 --> 00:07:29,531
Yeah, I'm pretty sure.

118
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:33,559
Galen was pretty sure
about the fumigation thing.

119
00:07:35,007 --> 00:07:36,998
Pink Floyd.

120
00:07:39,814 --> 00:07:43,683
- Mom, this is Dr. Gr-
- Gregory House.

121
00:07:43,752 --> 00:07:45,686
Nice to meet you.

122
00:07:45,755 --> 00:07:48,691
Would it be all right
if we spoke alone for a while?

123
00:07:48,759 --> 00:07:51,159
[Groans]
Well, you're gonna
need me to-

124
00:07:51,229 --> 00:07:53,561
I've got your case notes, Doctor.

125
00:07:53,632 --> 00:07:55,566
There's a cafeteria downstairs.

126
00:07:55,634 --> 00:07:57,568
Get yourself whatever you want,

127
00:07:57,637 --> 00:08:01,039
as long as there's enough left over
for a Reuben sandwich,
dry, no fries, hold the pickles.

128
00:08:01,109 --> 00:08:03,543
Should run you
about 5.80 with tax.

129
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,211
I'll page you when we're done.

130
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:17,056
No...

131
00:08:17,131 --> 00:08:19,361
pickles.

132
00:08:24,507 --> 00:08:26,498
Nice kid.

133
00:08:28,011 --> 00:08:30,445
How much do you really drink?

134
00:08:37,124 --> 00:08:39,991
He's really talking to a patient?

135
00:08:40,061 --> 00:08:42,997
I don't know who I am anymore.

136
00:08:43,065 --> 00:08:46,001
It's a blood clot.
What's so fascinating about that?

137
00:08:46,069 --> 00:08:49,836
He likes crazy people.
He likes the way they think.

138
00:08:49,908 --> 00:08:52,844
They think badly.
That's the definition of crazy.

139
00:08:52,912 --> 00:08:55,676
Why would he like-
They're not boring.

140
00:08:55,749 --> 00:08:57,740
He likes that.

141
00:09:01,323 --> 00:09:03,257
And the meds?

142
00:09:03,326 --> 00:09:05,658
Baseball.
I like baseball.

143
00:09:07,665 --> 00:09:09,826
Very nice.
Very sad.

144
00:09:11,671 --> 00:09:15,335
My boy and me,
we went to see a game.

145
00:09:15,409 --> 00:09:18,846
Not "Mets."
Meds. Medicine.

146
00:09:22,618 --> 00:09:25,611
You take what
he tells you to take.

147
00:09:29,127 --> 00:09:31,118
No one believes me.

148
00:09:32,799 --> 00:09:34,790
I do.

149
00:09:41,811 --> 00:09:44,872
I thought
he liked rationality.

150
00:09:44,948 --> 00:09:46,882
He likes puzzles.

151
00:09:46,951 --> 00:09:49,078
Patients are puzzles?
You don't think so?

152
00:09:49,154 --> 00:09:51,679
I think they're people.
Yeah.

153
00:09:51,758 --> 00:09:56,321
Well, he hates them,
and he's fascinated by them.

154
00:09:57,799 --> 00:10:00,997
Tell me you can't relate
to that sentiment.

155
00:10:02,005 --> 00:10:04,998
[Laughing]

156
00:10:06,278 --> 00:10:09,646
You told Luke
it never hurt before.
Just rough.

157
00:10:09,715 --> 00:10:12,183
They didn't hurt.

158
00:10:12,252 --> 00:10:14,243
Didn't?

159
00:10:15,723 --> 00:10:18,055
Don't lie to him, Limpy.

160
00:10:18,127 --> 00:10:20,061
Lively Lucy never lies to Lucas.

161
00:10:20,129 --> 00:10:22,120
Look what I do to him.

162
00:10:24,802 --> 00:10:27,738
[Woman On P.A.]
Radiation therapist to Oncology.

163
00:10:27,806 --> 00:10:30,104
Learn anything
from the "human connection"?

164
00:10:30,176 --> 00:10:32,110
Yeah.
The Mets suck.

165
00:10:32,179 --> 00:10:37,516
Also, for the last two months,
she hasn't shaved her legs.
Because of the tremors, she cuts herself.

166
00:10:37,585 --> 00:10:40,247
- The tremors aren't new.
She must always cut herself.
- Exactly.

167
00:10:40,323 --> 00:10:42,257
Something changed
in the last two months.

168
00:10:42,326 --> 00:10:46,957
I'm thinking the amount of blood
when she cut herself.
So let's start with some blood work.

169
00:10:47,032 --> 00:10:50,730
Collect and send for clotting studies,
P.T., P.T.T., factor five, protein "C" and "S."

170
00:10:50,804 --> 00:10:52,931
The whole shebang.
Good luck.

171
00:10:54,208 --> 00:10:56,506
No pickles, and it's cold now.

172
00:10:56,578 --> 00:10:59,138
- If it's a Reuben,
that's the way he likes it.
- Everyone, this is Luke.

173
00:10:59,215 --> 00:11:02,151
- Allison Cameron. It's nice to finally meet you.
- Yeah. Save it. We're busy.

174
00:11:02,219 --> 00:11:05,986
Luke, give us another half hour
with your mom.
We need to do some tests.

175
00:11:09,830 --> 00:11:11,764
Nice kid.

176
00:11:11,832 --> 00:11:14,768
Take her off the psych meds.
That way we'll know what's what
on the physical side.

177
00:11:14,836 --> 00:11:18,295
And who knows,
we might get more out of her.

178
00:11:21,345 --> 00:11:23,336
Don't worry. No pickles.

179
00:11:24,716 --> 00:11:26,707
Happy birthday.

180
00:11:28,554 --> 00:11:31,182
Okay. Whose?

181
00:11:31,258 --> 00:11:35,525
I was going through your mail,
and it was on a form.
Happy birthday.

182
00:11:38,067 --> 00:11:40,001
Oh.

183
00:11:50,618 --> 00:11:52,552
No! No blood!
Not mine!

184
00:11:52,620 --> 00:11:54,554
For the test.
Dr. House told us-
[Spits]

185
00:11:54,623 --> 00:11:58,924
You're gonna steal it! Sell it!
No! No blood!

186
00:11:58,996 --> 00:12:02,433
No! No blood!
No! No blood!

187
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,434
Haldol, five milligrams, stat.

188
00:12:04,503 --> 00:12:07,837
No! No! No!

189
00:12:07,908 --> 00:12:11,436
No! No!
[Whimpering]

190
00:12:11,512 --> 00:12:14,345
No! No! Oh, no!

191
00:12:14,416 --> 00:12:17,078
[Sighs]
N-No!

192
00:12:17,153 --> 00:12:21,147
[Sighs, Moans]

193
00:12:21,225 --> 00:12:23,159
No.

194
00:12:23,228 --> 00:12:26,221
[Moaning]

195
00:12:28,969 --> 00:12:30,903
[Door Opens]

196
00:12:30,972 --> 00:12:34,465
Well, good news.
The lab says it's not strep,
so we're done.

197
00:12:34,543 --> 00:12:37,479
Wait a second.
No, really not strep.

198
00:12:37,547 --> 00:12:40,949
The boys in the lab, sure,
they're hard drinkers,
but they're pros, you know.

199
00:12:41,018 --> 00:12:43,578
Plus, your kid actually has none
of the symptoms for strep.

200
00:12:43,655 --> 00:12:46,591
It was quicker running the test
than arguing with you.
My point is, go!

201
00:12:46,659 --> 00:12:49,595
I just wanted to ask
your opinion, Doctor.

202
00:12:49,663 --> 00:12:52,599
She's having a birthday party next week,
and she's upset that
I'm getting a sugarless cake.

203
00:12:52,667 --> 00:12:55,603
The other kids hate it.
This is why you're here.

204
00:12:55,671 --> 00:12:58,607
Sugar is the leading cause
of obesity in America.

205
00:12:58,675 --> 00:13:01,611
You want a doctor to scare her
about the dangers of sugar.

206
00:13:01,679 --> 00:13:04,615
She needs to get her weight
under control.

207
00:13:04,683 --> 00:13:10,122
Well, you know, I feel sorry
for those other kids, Wendy,
who don't have a mom like yours-

208
00:13:10,191 --> 00:13:14,629
a mom who knows
that sugar causes heart disease,
appendicitis and athlete's foot.

209
00:13:14,697 --> 00:13:17,633
That's not fair.
Oh, yes, it is.
No, I get it.

210
00:13:17,701 --> 00:13:21,138
You want her to slim down a little
so she can wear pretty clothes like yours.

211
00:13:21,206 --> 00:13:23,140
Love the bracelets.

212
00:13:23,209 --> 00:13:26,145
Hey, what about matching outfits?
You could be twins.

213
00:13:26,213 --> 00:13:28,613
[Gasps]
She can't be your daughter.

214
00:13:28,683 --> 00:13:31,208
It's impossible!
You look way too young.

215
00:13:31,287 --> 00:13:33,221
Happy birthday.

216
00:13:33,289 --> 00:13:35,223
Get the kid
a damned ice cream cake.

217
00:13:35,292 --> 00:13:38,421
[Woman on P.A.]
Dr. Wyland to Obstetrics.
Dr. Wyland, Obstetrics.

218
00:13:38,496 --> 00:13:41,660
- You drugged her.
- Actually, I didn't.

219
00:13:41,733 --> 00:13:44,293
I've taken her off
all medication.
Your guy Foreman gave her Haldol.

220
00:13:44,370 --> 00:13:48,034
We needed blood for tests.
I assumed that was
the only way to get it.

221
00:13:48,109 --> 00:13:52,171
He knocked her out.
Look, I have a cane,
and I know how to use it.

222
00:13:52,248 --> 00:13:54,239
I hired you.
You work for me.

223
00:13:54,318 --> 00:13:55,945
Okay.

224
00:13:56,019 --> 00:13:58,044
Can I go now, boss?

225
00:14:01,226 --> 00:14:03,160
The Haldol changes her.

226
00:14:03,229 --> 00:14:07,564
She says it makes her soul numb.
Don't give it to her.

227
00:14:12,108 --> 00:14:14,042
"If there be rags enough,

228
00:14:14,111 --> 00:14:17,946
he will know her name
and be well pleased remembering it."

229
00:14:18,016 --> 00:14:20,678
[Coughing]

230
00:14:20,753 --> 00:14:23,187
You okay?

231
00:14:23,256 --> 00:14:25,247
Old days.

232
00:14:28,397 --> 00:14:30,331
"For in the old days,

233
00:14:30,399 --> 00:14:33,335
"though she had young men's praise
and old men's blame,

234
00:14:33,403 --> 00:14:36,373
among the poor,
both old and young gave her praise."

235
00:14:36,440 --> 00:14:38,670
[Coughs]

236
00:14:39,711 --> 00:14:41,872
[Groaning]
Oh!

237
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:48,211
Help! Somebody help!

238
00:14:53,196 --> 00:14:58,567
So, when I said no psych meds,
I'm just curious-
which word didn't you understand?

239
00:14:58,637 --> 00:15:01,573
The Haldol had nothing to do
with the bleed.

240
00:15:01,641 --> 00:15:04,508
You know that.
I used it purely as a chemical restraint.

241
00:15:04,578 --> 00:15:06,739
Oh, great.
Well, that's good to hear.

242
00:15:06,815 --> 00:15:11,446
So she won't experience any
of those pesky little side effects you get
when your motives aren't pure.

243
00:15:11,521 --> 00:15:13,751
Oh-
Those side effects are so rare!

244
00:15:13,824 --> 00:15:17,591
What, passing out,
increased confusion, depression?
That's not gonna happen.

245
00:15:17,663 --> 00:15:21,725
That's not gonna screw up our diagnosis,
'cause you just used it to restrain her.
I'm so relieved.

246
00:15:21,802 --> 00:15:23,736
She spit in my face.

247
00:15:23,805 --> 00:15:26,741
[House]
That must have been
so frightening for you.

248
00:15:26,809 --> 00:15:29,745
What was I supposed to do,
tie her down?
Yeah. Anything but give her drugs.

249
00:15:29,813 --> 00:15:31,747
That's basically my point.

250
00:15:31,816 --> 00:15:36,379
The clotting studies. Pretty fast.
Did you promise to date the entire lab?

251
00:15:36,455 --> 00:15:38,821
No, I save that for emergencies.

252
00:15:38,891 --> 00:15:42,259
I told them she bled out two units
and if it happened again, she'd die.

253
00:15:42,329 --> 00:15:45,265
If it had happened at home,
she would have died.

254
00:15:45,333 --> 00:15:48,097
The E.R. Doc,
he was gonna send her home.

255
00:15:48,170 --> 00:15:51,572
- I used my bestjudgment.
- It turns out your best judgment
is not good enough.

256
00:15:51,641 --> 00:15:55,100
- Here's an idea- next time, use mine.
- I think they're choosing a movie.

257
00:15:55,180 --> 00:15:57,944
Why did the patient bleed out?

258
00:15:58,017 --> 00:16:00,611
The clotting studies so far
are normal.

259
00:16:00,688 --> 00:16:03,624
Well, cover your ears if you don't
want me to spoil the ending.

260
00:16:03,692 --> 00:16:07,356
Everything was normal,
except for a prolonged P.T. Time.

261
00:16:07,430 --> 00:16:11,925
- Which means what?
- Usually it means whoever
drew the blood didn't do it right.

262
00:16:12,002 --> 00:16:14,334
Oh, that's right,
'cause you drew the blood.

263
00:16:14,405 --> 00:16:18,866
- But you were precise because you knew
the tube was purely for the P.T. Study.
- That's right.

264
00:16:18,945 --> 00:16:22,382
And I'm right with you.
I trust this result,
for two reasons-

265
00:16:22,450 --> 00:16:25,010
"A," because you are
a good doctor,

266
00:16:25,087 --> 00:16:27,817
and "B," because
five milligrams of I.V. Haldol...

267
00:16:27,891 --> 00:16:30,827
makes for a spectacularly
cooperative patient.

268
00:16:32,229 --> 00:16:35,495
The prolonged P.T. Time
makes me think she's got
a vitamin "K" deficiency.

269
00:16:35,568 --> 00:16:38,470
[Cameron]
Vitamin "K'"would explain the bleed,
but not the clot.

270
00:16:38,539 --> 00:16:41,907
Without vitamin "K,"
protein "C" doesn't work.
Without protein "C," she clots.

271
00:16:41,976 --> 00:16:44,912
Clotting and thinning
all at the same time.

272
00:16:44,980 --> 00:16:48,348
What about another drug
interacting with Heparin,
an antibiotic like ampicillin?

273
00:16:48,418 --> 00:16:51,354
That would cause the bleed.
Clever,
but she's not on ampicillin.

274
00:16:51,422 --> 00:16:54,859
Two months ago,
she complained of a sore throat,
and he got her ampicillin.

275
00:16:54,927 --> 00:16:57,225
- Which she refused to take.
- Hejust said she didn't take it.

276
00:16:57,297 --> 00:17:00,495
What is it, everybody lies
except for schizophrenics
and their children?

277
00:17:00,568 --> 00:17:04,528
It's more likely than malnourishment.
Why not scurvy or the plague?

278
00:17:04,607 --> 00:17:07,167
Gee, I wish my idea
was as cool and with-it as yours.

279
00:17:07,244 --> 00:17:10,442
What is yours, by the way?
Do you have one?
Alcohol.

280
00:17:10,515 --> 00:17:14,316
Simple. It causes immobility,
which explains the D.V.T.

281
00:17:14,386 --> 00:17:19,586
It also causes cirrhosis,
which explains the bleed
and the prolonged P.T. Time.

282
00:17:19,660 --> 00:17:22,562
- Let's ultrasound the liver.
- [House] Three theories.

283
00:17:22,631 --> 00:17:25,897
Check out her place
for ampicillin and diet.

284
00:17:25,969 --> 00:17:27,903
Then ultrasound her liver.

285
00:17:27,972 --> 00:17:31,136
Let's find out who's right
before she bleeds to death.

286
00:17:45,328 --> 00:17:47,319
101.

287
00:17:54,607 --> 00:17:58,977
So House says the kid is sensitive,
thinks he takes good care of her.

288
00:17:59,047 --> 00:18:02,245
But if we don't find anything,
why let him know we did it in the first place?

289
00:18:02,318 --> 00:18:04,252
What's the point?

290
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:09,054
Why not just make old Foreman
lift the key from the kid's backpack?

291
00:18:23,813 --> 00:18:26,475
Looks like Luke
sleeps in the living room.

292
00:18:33,794 --> 00:18:36,319
Nothing in there.

293
00:18:36,397 --> 00:18:38,331
He lays out her clothes?

294
00:18:38,399 --> 00:18:42,530
Enough organization, enough lists,
you think you can control
the uncontrollable.

295
00:18:42,605 --> 00:18:46,667
Fix her meds, fix her clothes,
maybe you can even fix her.

296
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,414
Pick that up
on your psych rotation?

297
00:19:00,062 --> 00:19:03,464
Trifluoperazine, Thorazine, Clozarile.

298
00:19:03,533 --> 00:19:05,831
Whew.
They tried everything.

299
00:19:05,903 --> 00:19:10,170
The ampicillin-
never touched it.

300
00:19:10,242 --> 00:19:14,407
There goes Cameron's theory.
Oh, God, I hope it's not
a vitamin "K" deficiency.

301
00:19:21,657 --> 00:19:24,285
[Sighs]
Damn.

302
00:19:24,361 --> 00:19:26,295
Breakfast, lunch and dinner.

303
00:19:26,364 --> 00:19:28,298
House was right.

304
00:19:28,367 --> 00:19:30,358
[Beeping]

305
00:19:31,371 --> 00:19:33,805
That's the only thing
she'll eat.
Yeah.

306
00:19:33,875 --> 00:19:36,811
The problem is,
you can't actually live on this stuff.

307
00:19:36,879 --> 00:19:40,816
I checked it out. I looked on the box.
All the nutritional values are solid.

308
00:19:40,884 --> 00:19:42,818
There's plenty
of protein and calories.

309
00:19:42,887 --> 00:19:46,915
Yeah, vitamin "A" and "C," but no "K."
That's why your mom got sick.

310
00:19:49,462 --> 00:19:52,626
So what's the plan then?
Load her up with vitamin "K."

311
00:19:52,699 --> 00:19:57,034
That's it?
If it all checks out, you can
take her home in a couple of days.

312
00:19:58,841 --> 00:20:00,775
[Sighs]

313
00:20:00,844 --> 00:20:03,335
Oh, God, you're upset
about something.

314
00:20:04,615 --> 00:20:08,347
You're gonna open up to me now,
aren't you?
It's all my fault.

315
00:20:09,489 --> 00:20:11,423
Here we go.

316
00:20:11,492 --> 00:20:15,486
Okay, I'm gonna say this once:
You have done a very good job
taking care of your mother.

317
00:20:15,564 --> 00:20:19,763
If this was all she'd eat,
then what else could you do?
Gosh, just being a kid is a full-time job.

318
00:20:19,836 --> 00:20:23,830
Shut up! I'm 18.
I should be able
to take care of my mom.

319
00:20:25,210 --> 00:20:28,442
I almost killed her.
Good example.

320
00:20:28,514 --> 00:20:33,452
Just the time it takes
to express those ridiculous,
self-centered teenage ideas.

321
00:20:33,521 --> 00:20:35,580
I don't envy your schedule.

322
00:20:37,927 --> 00:20:40,293
No pickles.

323
00:20:40,364 --> 00:20:42,491
My mom
doesn't like 'em either.

324
00:20:42,566 --> 00:20:44,557
Smart woman.

325
00:20:45,770 --> 00:20:48,706
Before she got sick,
I didn't like how bossy she was.

326
00:20:48,775 --> 00:20:52,371
Always telling me what to do,
the right way to do it.

327
00:20:53,214 --> 00:20:56,844
[Chuckles]
Never thought I'd miss that.

328
00:20:56,919 --> 00:21:00,549
Oww.
[Groans]

329
00:21:02,226 --> 00:21:04,251
You should get that looked at.

330
00:21:04,329 --> 00:21:07,265
I still don't buy
a vitamin "K" deficiency.
House was right.

331
00:21:07,333 --> 00:21:10,269
That usually makes you happy.
Less work for us.

332
00:21:10,337 --> 00:21:13,865
The kid feeds his mom
a steady diet of booze,
and the problem is too many burgers?

333
00:21:13,942 --> 00:21:16,878
The kid's in a tough situation.
You do what you gotta do to survive.

334
00:21:16,946 --> 00:21:20,383
Feeding alcohol to an alcoholic
is not a survival technique.

335
00:21:20,451 --> 00:21:22,783
Where I come from, if it works-
Yeah, right.

336
00:21:22,854 --> 00:21:26,848
I'm rich.
I couldn't possibly understand
what this kid is going through.

337
00:21:28,061 --> 00:21:32,499
Just because you're drinking
pricier stuff doesn't mean
you don't have a problem.

338
00:21:32,568 --> 00:21:35,504
You've seen someone
stagger down that road?

339
00:21:35,572 --> 00:21:38,508
No way vitamin "K"
is the whole story.

340
00:21:43,114 --> 00:21:46,050
[Sighs]
It's not broken.

341
00:21:46,118 --> 00:21:48,052
See this, right here?

342
00:21:48,121 --> 00:21:52,422
It's the epiphyseal plate,
otherwise known as the "growth plate."
What's wrong with it?

343
00:21:52,494 --> 00:21:55,156
Amazing thing, this bone.

344
00:21:55,232 --> 00:21:58,259
If you know how to read it,
it can tell you how old someone really is.

345
00:21:58,336 --> 00:22:01,568
- Exactly how old.
- Great.

346
00:22:01,640 --> 00:22:04,473
Mmm, not even 15.

347
00:22:04,544 --> 00:22:06,478
Almost though.

348
00:22:06,547 --> 00:22:09,311
Two weeks away.
Maybe a month.

349
00:22:10,852 --> 00:22:14,084
Last week.
I was 15 last week.

350
00:22:14,156 --> 00:22:16,954
Happy birthday to both of us.

351
00:22:17,027 --> 00:22:19,860
If you're gonna lie though, go big.
Go 21.

352
00:22:19,931 --> 00:22:22,867
That way
you won't need your crazy mom
to help you buy vodka.

353
00:22:22,935 --> 00:22:26,428
Uh, great.
Thanks for the tip.

354
00:22:28,042 --> 00:22:32,742
Now, when I bring my mom home,
is there anything I need to know
about taking care of her?

355
00:22:32,815 --> 00:22:35,751
I suppose your biggest worry
isn't the booze.

356
00:22:35,819 --> 00:22:38,755
You're 15, basically no mom.

357
00:22:38,823 --> 00:22:41,451
If Child Welfare let kids get away
with that, they wouldn't need
those nice foster homes,

358
00:22:41,527 --> 00:22:43,461
and that would make them sad.

359
00:22:43,529 --> 00:22:45,429
They'd put her someplace too.

360
00:22:45,499 --> 00:22:47,490
My life is working.

361
00:22:48,569 --> 00:22:51,037
Not the word I'd use.

362
00:22:51,106 --> 00:22:54,668
Most 15-year-old kids are doing
what they're supposed to be doing.

363
00:22:54,744 --> 00:22:56,678
You know, they're huffing glue,
catching crabs.

364
00:22:56,747 --> 00:22:59,375
If you turn me in, I'll sue you.

365
00:22:59,451 --> 00:23:01,646
That's privileged information.

366
00:23:01,721 --> 00:23:04,246
Oh, relax.
It's not even your X-ray.

367
00:23:08,496 --> 00:23:10,430
She's awfully calm.

368
00:23:10,499 --> 00:23:12,490
House wrote new orders.

369
00:23:16,607 --> 00:23:19,371
There's a little bit of scarring.
Not much.

370
00:23:19,445 --> 00:23:21,379
Not enough to-
It's cirrhosis.

371
00:23:21,447 --> 00:23:23,540
Oh, but she doesn't drink.

372
00:23:23,616 --> 00:23:25,607
Congratulations.
You win.

373
00:23:28,256 --> 00:23:30,247
Actually,

374
00:23:31,527 --> 00:23:33,461
no one wins.

375
00:23:33,530 --> 00:23:36,466
Tumor. Cystic?

376
00:23:36,534 --> 00:23:38,900
[Cameron]
Solid mass.

377
00:23:38,971 --> 00:23:41,030
Cancer.

378
00:23:42,442 --> 00:23:46,880
The vitamin "K'"caused the D. V.T.
And aggravated the liver,

379
00:23:46,948 --> 00:23:49,884
but the tumor's
the real reason for the bleed.

380
00:23:49,952 --> 00:23:52,113
The tumor's the problem.

381
00:23:52,188 --> 00:23:54,656
Mrs. Palmero, I'm Dr. Wilson.

382
00:23:54,725 --> 00:23:58,355
I'm afraid I have some bad news
from your ultrasound.

383
00:23:58,430 --> 00:24:00,364
You have cancer.

384
00:24:00,433 --> 00:24:02,924
[No Audible Dialogue]

385
00:24:33,912 --> 00:24:36,312
It's big.
5.8 centimeters.

386
00:24:36,382 --> 00:24:39,112
We do nothing,
she dies from liver failure
within 60 days.

387
00:24:39,185 --> 00:24:41,619
- She needs a transplant.
- That's gonna happen.

388
00:24:41,688 --> 00:24:46,058
- She's 38 years old. She's a mother.
- She's a schizophrenic mother with no money,
on the public dole, in fact,

389
00:24:46,128 --> 00:24:48,562
who knocks back vodka
every time a breeze blows her way.

390
00:24:48,631 --> 00:24:51,897
Mickey Mantle had
a whole bar named after him.
He got a transplant.

391
00:24:51,969 --> 00:24:53,903
Yeah, well, Lucy can't switch-hit.

392
00:24:53,972 --> 00:24:56,338
Plan "B"-
surgery to resect the tumor.

393
00:24:56,408 --> 00:25:00,209
Joe Bergin does the gamma knife thing.
Laser cauterizes while it cuts, saves more liver.

394
00:25:00,280 --> 00:25:02,976
The tumor's way too big.
He won't even consider it.

395
00:25:03,051 --> 00:25:06,419
Not a big risk-taker, Bergin.
Won't even drink milk
on its expiration date.

396
00:25:06,489 --> 00:25:09,253
He has no discretion.
5.8 centimeters is past
the surgical guidelines.

397
00:25:12,830 --> 00:25:15,162
Would he do it at 4.6?

398
00:25:15,234 --> 00:25:20,263
Why don't we just say it's zero?
Then we don't need him at all.
Tumors grow, they don't shrink.

399
00:25:20,341 --> 00:25:22,332
This one does.

400
00:25:23,778 --> 00:25:26,714
Ninety-five percent ethanol.

401
00:25:26,782 --> 00:25:30,048
The ethanol dehydrates
the tumor cells.

402
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:32,111
Literally sucks them dry.

403
00:25:33,992 --> 00:25:35,926
It shrinks the tumor temporarily.

404
00:25:35,995 --> 00:25:37,986
How temporarily?

405
00:25:39,332 --> 00:25:41,823
Well, if we're lucky,

406
00:25:44,706 --> 00:25:47,699
just long enough to fool the surgeon.

407
00:25:54,820 --> 00:25:57,984
[Woman On P.A.]
Dr. Shopius, report to Psych Exam.

408
00:25:58,058 --> 00:26:01,290
Dr. Shopius, Psych Exam.

409
00:26:01,362 --> 00:26:03,296
Good morning, Dr. House.

410
00:26:03,365 --> 00:26:05,299
Good morning, Dr. Cuddy.

411
00:26:05,367 --> 00:26:07,301
Love that outfit.

412
00:26:07,370 --> 00:26:09,998
It says, " I'm professional,
but I'm still a woman."

413
00:26:10,074 --> 00:26:12,508
Actually, it sort of yells
the second part.

414
00:26:12,578 --> 00:26:15,514
Yeah, and your big cane
is real subtle too.

415
00:26:15,582 --> 00:26:19,713
Gotta go. Those running noses aren't
just gonna start walking on their own.

416
00:26:19,787 --> 00:26:22,312
The clinic can wait.

417
00:26:22,390 --> 00:26:24,950
How long?
Maybe we could catch a movie.

418
00:26:25,027 --> 00:26:28,122
You should know by now
my doctors have no secrets from me.

419
00:26:29,567 --> 00:26:31,501
I don't believe it.

420
00:26:31,569 --> 00:26:34,060
Who came running to Mommy?

421
00:26:34,139 --> 00:26:37,132
It doesn't matter who.
The point is, I know exactly what you did.

422
00:26:38,813 --> 00:26:41,338
You have no idea
what I'm talking about.

423
00:26:41,416 --> 00:26:45,353
Somebody knows about a bad thing you did.
That's a big field.

424
00:26:45,421 --> 00:26:49,654
But somebody you think might've told me.
That narrows it down quite a bit.

425
00:26:49,728 --> 00:26:52,664
Someone who views me
as a maternal authority figure.

426
00:26:52,732 --> 00:26:56,066
Ayoung person perhaps?
How am I doing?
Think I'm gonna get there?

427
00:26:56,136 --> 00:26:59,072
Presumably hospital business.
How many patients-
It's Cameron.

428
00:26:59,140 --> 00:27:01,074
She found out about my birthday.

429
00:27:01,143 --> 00:27:06,081
I thought she told you and I'd have to
stand here and smile while you gave me
a sweatshirt or a fruit basket.

430
00:27:06,149 --> 00:27:08,549
You know, made me feel
that deep sense of belonging.

431
00:27:08,619 --> 00:27:13,182
Actually, I was just gonna remind you,
you owe me six clinic hours this week.

432
00:27:13,259 --> 00:27:15,250
Oops.

433
00:27:22,171 --> 00:27:24,105
Hi, this is Dr. Cuddy.

434
00:27:24,174 --> 00:27:28,168
I need all the charts
on Dr. House's current patients.

435
00:27:30,015 --> 00:27:31,915
[Hiccup]

436
00:27:31,984 --> 00:27:34,920
I've tried everything.
Mm-hmm.

437
00:27:34,988 --> 00:27:39,221
"Pulling the tongue,
ice packs on the throat, hitting yourself,

438
00:27:39,294 --> 00:27:41,524
the groin pinch."

439
00:27:42,665 --> 00:27:46,193
Well, you've certainly covered
all the normal medical bases.

440
00:27:46,270 --> 00:27:49,206
Uh, how are you hitting yourself
though?

441
00:27:49,274 --> 00:27:51,936
Is it with an open hand
or a fist?
Open hand.

442
00:27:52,011 --> 00:27:54,947
Uh-huh. Well, that's-
that's how they teach it
at Harvard Med.

443
00:27:55,015 --> 00:27:57,006
How hard though?

444
00:27:59,354 --> 00:28:01,879
I'm sorry. I missed that.
Could you do that again?

445
00:28:01,958 --> 00:28:03,550
Uh-

446
00:28:03,626 --> 00:28:05,560
That's-That's very good.

447
00:28:05,629 --> 00:28:07,563
[Hiccup]
It's hiccups.

448
00:28:07,632 --> 00:28:10,066
I need to
speak with you now.
Mm-hmm.

449
00:28:10,136 --> 00:28:12,070
I need to go pee-pee.

450
00:28:12,138 --> 00:28:14,936
Dial it up a notch and repeat.
I'll be back.

451
00:28:24,689 --> 00:28:27,625
Ooh.
Girl in the boy's bathroom.

452
00:28:27,693 --> 00:28:29,627
Very dramatic.

453
00:28:29,695 --> 00:28:32,688
It must be very important,
what she has to say to me.

454
00:28:34,368 --> 00:28:37,304
Yesterday your patient's tumor
was 5.8 centimeters.

455
00:28:37,372 --> 00:28:39,897
Today it's 4.6.
How does that happen?

456
00:28:39,976 --> 00:28:43,413
Well, at a guess,
I'd say, " That Dr. House
must be really, really good.

457
00:28:43,480 --> 00:28:45,414
Why am I wasting him
on hiccups?"

458
00:28:45,483 --> 00:28:47,474
I wash before and after.

459
00:28:50,090 --> 00:28:53,390
You also requisitioned
20 c.c.'s of ethanol.

460
00:28:53,461 --> 00:28:56,397
What patient was that for?
Or are you planning a party?

461
00:28:56,465 --> 00:28:58,456
Do me a favor.

462
00:29:00,670 --> 00:29:03,332
I was gonna say leave,
but that works.

463
00:29:04,543 --> 00:29:07,479
- You shrunk the tumor.
- Only way to get the guy
to do the surgery.

464
00:29:07,547 --> 00:29:11,177
Fraud was the only way?
[Sighs]

465
00:29:11,251 --> 00:29:14,187
There is a reason
that we have these guidelines.

466
00:29:14,255 --> 00:29:17,191
I know. To save lives.
Specifically, doctors' lives.

467
00:29:17,259 --> 00:29:20,195
And not just their lives,
but their lifestyles.

468
00:29:20,263 --> 00:29:23,700
Wouldn't want to operate
on anyone really sick.
They might die and spoil our stats.

469
00:29:23,768 --> 00:29:26,362
Bergin has a right to know
what he is operating on.
True.

470
00:29:26,438 --> 00:29:29,567
I got all focused
on her right to live and forgot.
You do what you think is right.

471
00:29:39,656 --> 00:29:41,590
You really didn't know?

472
00:29:41,658 --> 00:29:44,286
No, I didn't.

473
00:29:44,362 --> 00:29:46,296
And, frankly, I'm angry.

474
00:29:46,365 --> 00:29:48,299
Which I'm guessing
is the correct response.

475
00:29:48,368 --> 00:29:51,736
- Of course, I'll know better once
you tell me what you're talking about.
- Your birthday.

476
00:29:51,806 --> 00:29:53,740
Oh.

477
00:29:53,809 --> 00:29:56,745
Anger was a bad guess.

478
00:29:56,813 --> 00:30:01,911
Well, normally, I'd put on a festive hat
and celebrate the fact that the Earth
has circled the sun one more time.

479
00:30:01,987 --> 00:30:04,923
I really didn't think
it was gonna make it this year,

480
00:30:04,991 --> 00:30:08,393
but darn it if it wasn't
the little planet that could
all over again.

481
00:30:08,462 --> 00:30:11,830
It's a birthday.
It's an excuse to be happy.

482
00:30:11,900 --> 00:30:13,891
You think that's lame?

483
00:30:15,471 --> 00:30:18,464
Why are you here?
Did you buy me a pony?

484
00:30:20,511 --> 00:30:22,502
I'm just waiting for the surgery.

485
00:30:24,549 --> 00:30:27,211
Yeah, well, go scrub in.

486
00:30:38,068 --> 00:30:41,163
All right, we're done.
Close her up.

487
00:30:46,079 --> 00:30:51,849
The tumor didn't just walk itself into a bar
and order up a double shot of ethanol.

488
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:54,354
Someone shrunk it down.

489
00:30:54,424 --> 00:30:57,861
I'm sorry.
It was very, very wrong.

490
00:30:57,928 --> 00:31:00,522
House is lucky
I didn't just close her up.

491
00:31:00,598 --> 00:31:03,534
He tries it again,
that's what happens.

492
00:31:03,602 --> 00:31:05,593
I'll pass it on.

493
00:31:08,543 --> 00:31:10,477
[Chase]
It looks like the surgeon got it all,

494
00:31:10,545 --> 00:31:13,241
but she's gonna have to have
some chemotherapy.

495
00:31:13,315 --> 00:31:15,749
[Luke]
What kind is it?
[Pencil Scratching]

496
00:31:15,819 --> 00:31:18,617
[Sighs]
Luke, stop writing.

497
00:31:25,833 --> 00:31:28,769
If you stop for a second,
it's not all gonna fall apart.

498
00:31:28,837 --> 00:31:31,271
Give yourself a break
once in a while.

499
00:31:31,340 --> 00:31:34,276
The fact is, your Mom's gonna have
an extra drink every now and then.

500
00:31:34,344 --> 00:31:36,369
No. No. She won't.
She doesn't.

501
00:31:36,446 --> 00:31:38,380
Fine.

502
00:31:38,449 --> 00:31:42,409
There are some things you just can't fix.
That's all I'm saying.

503
00:31:42,488 --> 00:31:46,858
That's how you'd handle it,
something like this?
You'd just give up?

504
00:31:48,229 --> 00:31:50,163
No.

505
00:31:50,232 --> 00:31:52,427
I'd do it just like you.

506
00:31:55,539 --> 00:31:58,805
It's an infusion.
She's gonna have a drain
in her abdomen.

507
00:31:58,877 --> 00:32:02,245
You're going to have to check
for possible infection.

508
00:32:02,314 --> 00:32:04,248
[Woman]
Lucas Palmero?

509
00:32:04,317 --> 00:32:07,810
Trina Wyatt, Child Services,
State of NewJersey.

510
00:32:07,889 --> 00:32:09,823
Can I help you?
This is a private room.

511
00:32:09,892 --> 00:32:12,828
He's only 15 years old,
a minor.

512
00:32:12,896 --> 00:32:14,830
He's in a tough living situation.

513
00:32:14,899 --> 00:32:16,833
We're just here to help.

514
00:32:16,901 --> 00:32:19,699
I don't need your help.
Fifteen?

515
00:32:19,771 --> 00:32:24,436
Lucas, you're gonna have to
come with us right now.

516
00:32:24,511 --> 00:32:26,172
Where are you taking him?

517
00:32:26,247 --> 00:32:29,649
Until the determination is made,
he'll be housed at Children's Services.

518
00:32:29,718 --> 00:32:33,280
I don't want to be "housed."
I live with my mom.

519
00:32:33,356 --> 00:32:35,847
Not for the next few days.

520
00:32:35,927 --> 00:32:39,193
Come on.
Let's not make this difficult, huh?

521
00:32:40,199 --> 00:32:43,066
Mom?

522
00:32:43,136 --> 00:32:45,127
Mom?

523
00:32:46,274 --> 00:32:48,265
I love you.

524
00:32:50,746 --> 00:32:54,478
When the Mets lost,
you remember?

525
00:32:54,551 --> 00:32:56,542
Yeah.

526
00:32:57,857 --> 00:32:59,882
I remember.

527
00:32:59,959 --> 00:33:02,519
I love you.

528
00:33:06,768 --> 00:33:09,032
[Stifles Sob]

529
00:33:13,010 --> 00:33:15,706
[Sniffling]

530
00:33:21,788 --> 00:33:25,225
Cuddy didn't say anything about
pushing Bergin to finish the surgery?
Not a word.

531
00:33:25,293 --> 00:33:28,229
Some kind of mind game.
She's waiting for me to crack.

532
00:33:28,297 --> 00:33:31,529
Well, either that
or she's just being nice.
Yeah, right.

533
00:33:31,601 --> 00:33:34,935
You said you wouldn't call.
You're a real bastard, you know.

534
00:33:39,012 --> 00:33:42,574
Yeah, I get that a lot.

535
00:33:46,789 --> 00:33:49,280
I don't think Mom's crazy.

536
00:33:54,098 --> 00:33:58,934
"For in the old days,
though she had young men's praise
and old men's blame,

537
00:33:59,005 --> 00:34:03,340
among the poor,
both old and young gave her praise."

538
00:34:09,987 --> 00:34:12,581
You called Social Services.

539
00:34:12,656 --> 00:34:14,590
It was you.

540
00:34:14,659 --> 00:34:17,184
N- No. No.

541
00:34:17,263 --> 00:34:19,823
It's okay, it's okay.
I get it.

542
00:34:21,235 --> 00:34:24,171
He'll have an easier time
dealing with the system.

543
00:34:24,240 --> 00:34:28,678
Sure, he won't be with his real mother,
but his real mother's sick.

544
00:34:28,745 --> 00:34:31,236
Someone needs to take care of him.

545
00:34:32,650 --> 00:34:34,584
I'm not gonna live here.

546
00:34:34,653 --> 00:34:40,024
What would his future have been,
taking you to chemo and back on the bus?

547
00:34:40,094 --> 00:34:43,064
And even if the cancer's
in complete remission,

548
00:34:43,131 --> 00:34:46,067
he'll still have a mother
who hears voices.

549
00:34:46,135 --> 00:34:49,594
Talk no more.

550
00:34:49,673 --> 00:34:51,607
Talk no more.

551
00:34:51,676 --> 00:34:55,704
"Look what I do to him, Limpy."

552
00:34:55,781 --> 00:34:58,306
You said that.

553
00:34:58,385 --> 00:35:00,649
I checked the phone records.

554
00:35:02,524 --> 00:35:05,084
Only one call from this room.

555
00:35:05,161 --> 00:35:08,097
Smart, because they
charge you two bucks a call.

556
00:35:08,165 --> 00:35:10,963
It was to Social Services,
the State of NewJersey.

557
00:35:11,036 --> 00:35:12,970
[Sighs]

558
00:35:13,038 --> 00:35:16,167
You're his mother.
You couldn't do it to him anymore.

559
00:35:19,414 --> 00:35:21,405
Good for you.

560
00:35:22,418 --> 00:35:24,511
Schizophrenics
can make rational decisions.

561
00:35:24,586 --> 00:35:29,149
On the small stuff, yeah-
when to sleep, what to drink, you know.

562
00:35:29,226 --> 00:35:31,922
"No lemonade,
but I'll take some hemlock
if you've got it."

563
00:35:31,997 --> 00:35:33,931
Huh.
Your man Socrates.

564
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:39,132
But giving up your son
because it's better for him,
it's so sane, it's so rational.

565
00:35:39,207 --> 00:35:42,143
Self-sacrifice is not
a symptom of schizophrenia.

566
00:35:42,211 --> 00:35:44,145
It excludes the diagnosis.

567
00:35:44,213 --> 00:35:47,149
She's not schizophrenic?
Look, she's 36 years old
when she first presents.

568
00:35:47,217 --> 00:35:49,651
It's a little late,
but within the parameters.

569
00:35:49,721 --> 00:35:53,351
The internist sends her to a shrink.
One shrink sends her to the next.

570
00:35:53,425 --> 00:35:56,725
She tells them all she's not crazy.
The drugs don't work.
And why would they if she's not a head case?

571
00:35:56,797 --> 00:35:59,561
She got clearer
when I took her off the psych meds.

572
00:36:01,903 --> 00:36:05,340
You think I'm crazy.
Well, yeah,
but that's not the problem.

573
00:36:05,409 --> 00:36:08,504
Didn't we
just leave your office?
I like to walk.

574
00:36:10,883 --> 00:36:13,910
~ [Piano: Classical]

575
00:36:18,025 --> 00:36:21,018
~ [Continues]

576
00:37:08,828 --> 00:37:11,764
Is that Dr. Jeffrey Walters?
Hi.

577
00:37:11,832 --> 00:37:14,699
My name is Greg House.
I'm a doctor-

578
00:37:15,703 --> 00:37:19,333
Oh, is that the time?
I'm-Yeah. I'm sorry.
My watch must have stopped.

579
00:37:19,408 --> 00:37:23,971
Uh, listen, you treated a patient
about 18 months ago,
a woman named Lucille Palmero.

580
00:37:24,048 --> 00:37:27,041
I wondered if you recalled
running any tests...

581
00:37:28,353 --> 00:37:29,980
at all.

582
00:37:30,723 --> 00:37:34,490
[British Accent]
Oh, how terribly foolish of me, Doctor.
Is it that late?

583
00:37:34,562 --> 00:37:38,226
Yes, I'm calling from London, you see?
Must have got my times mixed up-

584
00:37:45,810 --> 00:37:48,210
I have a headache.
It's my only symptom.

585
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:51,682
I go to see three doctors.
The neurologist tells me it's an aneurysm.

586
00:37:51,752 --> 00:37:53,947
The immunologist says
I've got hay fever.

587
00:37:54,021 --> 00:37:55,955
The intensivist can't be bothered.

588
00:37:56,024 --> 00:37:59,461
Sends me to a shrink,
who tells me that I'm punishing myself
'cause I want to sleep with my mommy.

589
00:37:59,529 --> 00:38:04,024
- Maybe you're just not getting enough sleep?
- Pick your specialist, you pick your disease.

590
00:38:04,102 --> 00:38:07,539
If it's not schizophrenia,
what else presents
with psych symptoms?

591
00:38:07,606 --> 00:38:10,200
- Porphyria.
- The Madness of King George.

592
00:38:10,277 --> 00:38:13,543
What about that copper thing?
What's it called? It's genetic.

593
00:38:13,614 --> 00:38:15,479
The body accumulates
too much copper.

594
00:38:15,551 --> 00:38:18,019
Oh, um, uh, Wilson's Disease?

595
00:38:18,087 --> 00:38:20,021
[Cameron]
Yeah.
Very rare.

596
00:38:20,089 --> 00:38:22,023
Nice. I like it.

597
00:38:22,092 --> 00:38:25,221
- If any of us did this, you'd fire us.
- Well, that's funny.

598
00:38:25,297 --> 00:38:28,324
I thought I encouraged you
to question.
You're not questioning.

599
00:38:28,401 --> 00:38:30,835
You're hoping.
You want it to be Wilson's.

600
00:38:30,904 --> 00:38:33,168
Boom. Give her
a couple of drugs, she's okay.

601
00:38:33,241 --> 00:38:35,266
July 17.
An appointment with a Dr. Karn.

602
00:38:35,344 --> 00:38:38,906
She didn't keep it.
She never kept another appointment
with a shrink he made after that.

603
00:38:38,982 --> 00:38:42,180
Karn is not a shrink.
I looked him up.
He's an ophthalmologist.

604
00:38:42,253 --> 00:38:44,687
Now why would she
want her eyes checked?

605
00:38:44,756 --> 00:38:47,088
Wilson's presents with cataracts,
I think.
Yes, it does.

606
00:38:47,160 --> 00:38:52,428
It also causes slight cirrhosis,
which Dr. Chase so eagerly
attributed to alcohol.

607
00:38:52,500 --> 00:38:54,934
So what are we still doing here?

608
00:38:55,003 --> 00:38:56,630
Lucy.!

609
00:38:56,706 --> 00:38:59,266
I don't think you're crazy.

610
00:38:59,343 --> 00:39:02,141
[Sighs]
Neither do I.

611
00:39:02,213 --> 00:39:05,547
But I'm crazy.
Come on.
Lean over here.

612
00:39:05,618 --> 00:39:07,609
Come on.

613
00:39:09,022 --> 00:39:10,956
[House]
That's it.

614
00:39:11,025 --> 00:39:15,122
Put your hands on the bar here
and your chin in here.

615
00:39:15,197 --> 00:39:17,131
Thank you.

616
00:39:17,200 --> 00:39:19,566
You're gonna see
a bright light, okay?

617
00:39:19,637 --> 00:39:22,573
Your body might be accumulating
too much copper.

618
00:39:22,641 --> 00:39:26,009
If it is, this should help us
see something
called Keyser-Fleischer rings-

619
00:39:27,214 --> 00:39:29,148
copper-colored circles
around your corneas.

620
00:39:29,216 --> 00:39:31,081
Wow.

621
00:39:32,655 --> 00:39:36,353
I guess we should start
treating her for Wilson's.

622
00:39:36,426 --> 00:39:38,519
That's what I'd do.

623
00:39:40,598 --> 00:39:43,591
[Monitor Beeping]

624
00:39:49,276 --> 00:39:53,543
[Lucy]
"I will talk no more
ofbooks or the long war,

625
00:39:53,616 --> 00:39:59,249
"but walk by the dry thorn
until I have found some beggar
sheltering from the wind...

626
00:39:59,323 --> 00:40:03,624
"and there manage the talk
until her name come round.

627
00:40:03,696 --> 00:40:09,101
If there be rags enough,
he will know her name
and be well pleased remembering it. '"

628
00:40:10,439 --> 00:40:15,673
"For in the old days,
though she had young men's praise
and old men's blame,

629
00:40:15,745 --> 00:40:20,115
among the poor,
both old and young gave her praise."

630
00:40:24,825 --> 00:40:26,816
[Sighs]

631
00:40:28,930 --> 00:40:31,490
Hi, Mrs. Palmero.
Ready to go home?

632
00:40:32,668 --> 00:40:34,602
Almost.

633
00:40:34,671 --> 00:40:36,901
Mom?

634
00:40:42,615 --> 00:40:44,606
[Sighs]

635
00:40:46,620 --> 00:40:48,417
How are you?

636
00:40:48,490 --> 00:40:51,254
I'm good.

637
00:40:54,331 --> 00:40:56,265
Oh!
[Laughs]

638
00:40:56,334 --> 00:40:58,859
Oh-

639
00:40:58,936 --> 00:41:01,872
Oh, you really need a haircut.

640
00:41:01,941 --> 00:41:04,375
[Both Laugh]

641
00:41:24,171 --> 00:41:26,162
[Bell Dings]

642
00:41:27,441 --> 00:41:29,432
Dr. House.

643
00:41:30,512 --> 00:41:32,503
Luke, we're making
Dr. House wait.

644
00:41:32,582 --> 00:41:35,107
That's okay.
We're just here for the music.

645
00:41:35,186 --> 00:41:37,518
Luke, come on.

646
00:41:48,404 --> 00:41:51,397
I'm being discharged.
I heard a rumor.

647
00:41:52,843 --> 00:41:55,175
Thank God I had cancer, huh?

648
00:41:56,380 --> 00:42:00,875
- It's terrible having everybody
think you're nuts.
- Really?

649
00:42:02,288 --> 00:42:05,918
- I called to thank you.
Did you get my message?
- Yes.

650
00:42:08,598 --> 00:42:10,566
You're welcome.

651
00:42:12,970 --> 00:42:15,963
I'm never thanking you.
You turned me in.

652
00:42:17,076 --> 00:42:21,173
I told you we were doing okay.
It was none of your business.

653
00:42:21,248 --> 00:42:23,648
Look, I don't care
how you were living.

654
00:42:23,718 --> 00:42:26,380
I just wanted you out of my life.

655
00:42:28,156 --> 00:42:30,886
That's why I had Dr. Cuddy
call Social Services.

656
00:42:34,266 --> 00:42:36,826
[Bell Dings]

657
00:42:47,816 --> 00:42:49,750
You okay?

658
00:42:49,819 --> 00:42:51,753
You were right.

659
00:42:51,822 --> 00:42:56,089
It wasn't the D.V.T.
It was the schizophrenia.
I know.

660
00:42:56,161 --> 00:42:58,493
She's not nearly
as interesting anymore.

661
00:42:59,999 --> 00:43:02,992
Isn't it your birthday
around now?

662
00:43:37,450 --> 00:43:38,712
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
